From 21c89c94e1ac48e03fe74f573bd7fc218b9f3b2b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 22:49:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix engine deadlock, permanent game loss, and false blunder
 verdicts

Five defects, found by auditing the analysis and app layers.

1. The Stockfish worker had a single response slot, so two overlapping
   evalPosition calls crossed handlers and the first promise never
   settled. Opening a game while "Analyze all" ran froze both, forever,
   with no timeout. Requests are now queued and run to completion one at
   a time, with a 120s timeout so a wedged worker rejects instead of
   hanging the UI. getEngine caches the promise, not the instance, so a
   startup race cannot build two engines.

2. syncGames wrote the in-progress month to IndexedDB but deliberately
   never read it back while it was current. Once the calendar rolled
   over, that partial snapshot became a "past" month and was trusted
   forever, so every game played after the last sync of a month was lost
   permanently. Cached months now carry a `complete` flag and a snapshot
   taken mid-month is refetched once the month ends. Entries written by
   the old code lack the flag and heal on the next sync.

3. Play mode set Skill Level on the shared engine and only restored it
   in finish(), so leaving a practice game part-way left game review
   running at Skill Level 6 - and wrote those weakened verdicts to the
   database. Strength is now a per-request option; analysis always
   searches at full strength.

4. explainMistake measured only the material the player lost, never
   subtracting what the recapture won back, so an even trade read as
   "about 3 points of material down" - on most flagged moves. It now
   measures the difference between both sides across the whole exchange,
   starting before the player's own move. The fork claim that ran after
   the recapture had already taken the forking piece off the board is
   gone; forks are only claimed where the forker survives.

5. classify never consulted bestUci before its threshold gates, so a
   forced only-move could be labelled a blunder and then recommended as
   the improvement one sentence later. A move the engine itself chose,
   or the only legal move, is now never the player's error, is not
   flagged, is not queued as a puzzle, and does not count toward ACPL.

Also: Chess.com 429s and 5xx are retried with backoff instead of
aborting a whole sync; puzzle grading and the try-a-move panel search at
the depth the game was analysed at rather than a hard-coded 12, so the
"loses about X pawns" figure is move quality and not depth delta; and
the try-a-move panel no longer silently gives up while a review is
running, since requests queue now.

